 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Fans of <em>The Office</em> might recognize the title Ester Manas and Balthazar Delepierre chose for the spring outing: “Somehow I Manage” is the memoir Michael Scott never quite gets around to writing. For those who follow the duo, it was a wink—they’ve been away from the PFW calendar for a couple of years now, but haplessness had nothing to do with it.</p><p>In that time, they walked away from wholesale, sold direct, posted their best numbers yet, and became parents. Cornélius turned one last week, and this was his first show: his burbling ran through a soundtrack that included the children’s classic “Buckeye Jim.”</p><p>That song was bookended by variations on the classic “I’m Every Woman,” an apt choice for a brand based on bodycon for all. The winning move here: now it’s mixed with more coverage and a charming haphazardness that anyone who has tried to pull it together and scramble out the door can relate to. It might be an improbable color combination, an oversized plaid shirt paired with a sheer skirt, a corseted bomber over leggings, something akin to rumpled pajamas, a roomy striped top over a beaded blue pencil skirt or a leopard coat thrown over whatever—a sartorial lifesaver if ever there was one. It all kinda shouldn’t have worked and yet it drew smiles from even jaded types.</p><p>The backbone of the story was, as ever, lingerie. A long-term partnership with the Belgian heritage house Primadonna, founded in 1865, underpinned many of these looks (some of the house’s seamstresses were in attendance, too). That ranged from dead stock finds like the 22,000 tiny bows embroidered on a skirt to faded hues to seriously solid construction: up close, the clothes displayed the kind of engineering that can only come from a place that understands how to fit 94 cup sizes. What these photos don’t convey is the confidence that oozed from every curve, jiggle and swagger.</p><p>At noon Paris time, three pieces from the collaboration went live. Ester Manas now plans to send out more capsules every six months, and pop-ups in Paris and Belgium are on the way. Time will tell whether those lingerie stirrup pants actually catch on but, overall, this felt like step forward. It was a charming outing that could resonate with “somehow-managing” women, whether they&#x27;re lingerie people or not.</p></div>",
